From ef7b356b5d128194a79040bd3ea6c314392e7444 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 22:51:34 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] system: a setting for the time that the clock of the computer
 keeps

LibreELEC Hades takes the clock of the computer (the RTC) to keep local
time, as Windows keeps it, and never writes it: /etc/adjtime of the image
says LOCAL, and systemd, connman, this add-on and setwakeup.sh go by it.
On a computer without Windows, whose clock keeps UTC, the time is then
off by the time zone until it is set over the network, and wake-ups for
recordings are programmed at the wrong hour. /etc/adjtime is in the
read-only image, so that could not be changed.

System > OS Configuration > "Hardware clock" chooses local time (the
default, as before) or UTC, from the next start. UTC writes
/storage/.cache/adjtime, an adjtime that says UTC: beside it first, then
renamed over it, synced, and read back by the rule of the init, which
puts an adjtime that says UTC over the one of the image for each start
while that file says so. Local time removes the file. The list says what
is chosen, and "from the next start" while that is not what holds now.
Before UTC is chosen it is said what that means on a computer that also
runs Windows, and asked, with "no" preselected; the list of the two has
what holds preselected, which is what Kodi answers when something else
closes it. In live mode, whose storage is in memory, nothing is changed,
and that is said.

Tested on the host, with the modules as they are and stand-ins for Kodi:
98 checks (the texts in the four languages, the rule on 17 kinds of
file, writing and removing, every answer of the dialogs) and 16
mutations, all caught. Not run in Kodi.
